{"name":"Glacier Point","hex":"#b3d8e5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glacier","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large body of ice which flows under its own mass, usually downhill.","An area of a mountain where snow is present year-round."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glacier"},{"term":"point","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small dot or mark.\nSomething tiny, as a pinprick; a very small mark. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Something tiny, as a pinprick; a very small mark. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Point taken; understood; got it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/point"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glacier Point","blocks":[{"text":"Glacier Point is a viewpoint above Yosemite Valley in the U.S. state of California. It is located on the south wall of Yosemite Valley at an elevation of 7,214 feet (2,199 m), 3,200 feet (980 m) above Curry Village. The point offers a superb view of several of Yosemite National Park's well-known landmarks, including Yosemite Valley, Yosemite Falls, Half Dome, Vernal Fall, Nevada Fall, and Clouds Rest. Between 1872 and 1968, it was the site of the Yosemite Firefall."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Glacier_Point_at_Sunset%2C_Yosemite_NP%2C_CA%2C_US_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Glacier_Point_at_Sunset%2C_Yosemite_NP%2C_CA%2C_US_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glacier_Point","wikidata":"Q3108318"},{"title":"Glacier","blocks":[{"text":"A glacier is a persistent body of natural ice, a form of\nrock, that is constantly moving under its own weight. A glacier forms where the accumulation of snow exceeds its ablation over many years, often centuries. It slowly flows and deforms under stresses induced by gravity, undergoing both ductile and brittle deformation, and acquiring distinguishing surface features, such as crevasses and seracs. As a glacier moves, it abrades underlying rock and debris to create glacial landforms, such as cirques, moraines, and fjords. Although a glacier may flow into a body of water, and can include ice shelves and ice tongues, it originates only on land and is distinct from the much thinner sea ice and lake ice that form on the surface of freezing bodies of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Geikie_Plateau_Glacier.JPG/330px-Geikie_Plateau_Glacier.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glacier","wikidata":"Q35666"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Man_op_Glacier_Point_Rock_in_Yosemite_Valley_Glacier_Point_Rock%2C_3201_feet._-_Yosemite_Valley._%28titel_op_object%29%2C_RP-F-2012-96-228.jpg/500px-Man_op_Glacier_Point_Rock_in_Yosemite_Valley_Glacier_Point_Rock%2C_3201_feet._-_Yosemite_Valley._%28titel_op_object%29%2C_RP-F-2012-96-228.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Man_op_Glacier_Point_Rock_in_Yosemite_Valley_Glacier_Point_Rock%2C_3201_feet._-_Yosemite_Valley._%28titel_op_object%29%2C_RP-F-2012-96-228.jpg/1280px-Man_op_Glacier_Point_Rock_in_Yosemite_Valley_Glacier_Point_Rock%2C_3201_feet._-_Yosemite_Valley._%28titel_op_object%29%2C_RP-F-2012-96-228.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Man_op_Glacier_Point_Rock_in_Yosemite_Valley_Glacier_Point_Rock%2C_3201_feet._-_Yosemite_Valley._%28titel_op_object%29%2C_RP-F-2012-96-228.jpg/500px-Man_op_Glacier_Point_Rock_in_Yosemite_Valley_Glacier_Point_Rock%2C_3201_feet._-_Yosemite_Valley._%28titel_op_object%29%2C_RP-F-2012-96-228.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Glac